{
  "dom_id": "step-q48504",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{ln\u2061(x)},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=ln\u2061x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2})^{\u2032}\\cdot ln\u2061x-{x}^{2}\\cdot (ln\u2061x)^{\u2032}}{(ln\u2061x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot ln\u2061(x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})}{{(ln\u2061(x))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot ln\u2061(x)-x}{{ln}^{2}\u2061(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2ln\u2061(x)-1)}{{ln}^{2}\u2061(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(e) & =\\frac{e(2ln\u2061(e)-1)}{{ln}^{2}\u2061(e)} \\\\  & =\\frac{e(2-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e.$",
